# Update these with your Supabase details from your project settings > API
# https://app.supabase.com/project/_/settings/api

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=""

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""

SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""

# Assets (Demos and Posters) are served from a Cloudflare R2 bucket published on
# a custom domain. Contributors only need the public base URL below; the site
# renders correctly from it with no Assets checked out.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CDN_URL="https://cdn.rnui.dev"

# The Firestore collections holding the view and vote counts, and the newsletter
# signups. Set both. Leaving the first unset falls back to "rnui" — the production
# collection — so a local run counts against real numbers; lib/counters-firestore.ts
# warns when that happens, but the fix is here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIRESTORE_COLLECTION="rnui-dev"
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIRESTORE_EMAIL_COLLECTION="emails-dev"

# public-submissions ticket 10 — the Submission consent records.
#
# Separate from the email collection and NOT catalogue data: a Submission never
# becomes a Recording except by hand, so nothing here is ever rendered. The rules
# deny every read, so this collection cannot publish who submitted what. See the
# `submissions` match block in firestore.rules.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIRESTORE_SUBMISSION_COLLECTION="submissions-dev"

# The PostHog project the browser reports into. `lib/posthog-provider.tsx` reads
# both of these; neither was documented here, so a fresh clone initialised
# posthog-js with `undefined` and captured nothing.
#
# notify-and-preview ticket 12: this is the one variable that must differ
# between rnui.dev and preview.rnui.dev. The Preview is the Studio Dark build
# and it reports into a PostHog project of its own, so that deploy A's six-week
# baseline on rnui.dev stays a measurement of the old site rather than a blend
# of both. On Vercel that means a Preview-scoped value, and the production one
# narrowed to Production — a key left on "All Environments" reaches the Preview
# too, and nothing about the resulting mixture looks wrong until deploy B is
# compared against it. `api_host` is hardcoded and is not an environment
# variable; see the comment at that call site before adding one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_UI_HOST="https://us.posthog.com"

# Maintainer only — uploads the production build's source maps to PostHog so
# error tracking resolves a stack trace to a real file and line. Leave the key
# blank, as it is here, and the upload is skipped; the site builds and behaves
# identically, which is what every contributor and fork wants. It is a personal
# API key with `error tracking: write`, from
# https://us.posthog.com/settings/user-api-keys — not the NEXT_PUBLIC_ token.
# Set both on Vercel, not just locally, or production ships minified traces.
POSTHOG_API_KEY=""
POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID="117415"

# Maintainer only — required by `pnpm assets:publish`, never by the site.
# Bucket: rnui-assets. Token needs Account > Workers R2 Storage > Edit.
# See docs/r2-setup.md.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=""
CLOUDFLARE_R2_TOKEN=""
R2_BUCKET="rnui-assets"

# public-submissions ticket 08 — the bucket Submissions land in.
#
# A SECOND bucket, not a prefix inside R2_BUCKET. Public access in R2 is a
# bucket-level setting with no per-prefix exclusion, so a `submissions/` prefix
# inside rnui-assets would have been world-readable at cdn.rnui.dev and cached
# immutable for a year. This bucket has no custom domain and its r2.dev managed
# domain is disabled, and `scripts/open-submission.ts` reads objects back out
# through the REST API instead. See docs/r2-setup.md.
#
# Optional: the tooling defaults to "rnui-submissions", so a pasted
# `pnpm submissions:open <key>` works from a shell that has never read this file.
R2_SUBMISSIONS_BUCKET="rnui-submissions"

# public-submissions ticket 07 — Cloudflare Turnstile.
#
# Three values. Two are a pair: a site key the browser widget carries (public,
# hence NEXT_PUBLIC_) and a secret the endpoint sends to Siteverify, which must
# never be public. The site key ships in every page load; only the secret is one.
#
# The widget EXISTS as of 2026-09-25 — sitekey 0x4AAAAAAFC4Sc7I0bVhAVA9, mode
# Managed. Its matching secret lives in the maintainer's own secret store and is
# deliberately not written down here. Note that an agent cannot read it back from
# the API either: CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN returns HTTP 403 against
# /accounts/{id}/challenges/widgets, verified 2026-09-25.
#
# On Vercel: NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY and TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES for all
# environments, TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY for Production and Preview only. Never give
# the secret a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix — not because that is how it would leak, but
# because a prefixed name is how it eventually gets read somewhere it should not.
#
# TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES is which hostnames a token is accepted FROM. That is a
# security boundary, not a convenience: production is `rnui.dev` alone. Add
# `localhost` in a LOCAL env file only — a hostname listed here is one whose token
# is accepted, so listing localhost in production accepts a token minted on
# somebody else's machine.
#
# For a fork, CI, or a build with no widget, Cloudflare's documented dummy pair
# needs nothing from the dashboard and works on any domain:
#
#   always passes  sitekey 1x00000000000000000000AA  secret 1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#   always fails   sitekey 2x00000000000000000000AB  secret 2x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#   duplicate                                    secret 3x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#   interactive    sitekey 3x00000000000000000000FF
#
# **But the dummy pair cannot exercise `/submit`.** Measured 2026-09-25: a dummy
# token through the always-pass secret returns success carrying
# `"hostname":"example.com"` and **no `action` field at all** —
#
#   {"challenge_ts":"…","error-codes":[],"hostname":"example.com",
#    "metadata":{"result_with_testing_key":true},"success":true}
#
# — and lib/turnstile.ts refuses any response whose action is not SUBMIT_ACTION. So
# every dummy-token submission is rejected as `action-mismatch`, the visitor is told
# "We could not verify the browser check", and nothing tells them why. A fork using
# the dummy pair gets a `/submit` that refuses everyone: correct, and silent.
#
# No way round it keeps the check worth having — accepting the dummy hostname means
# allowlisting `example.com` in production, and accepting a missing action means not
# binding the token to this surface. Both are what ticket 03's research says not to
# do. To exercise `/submit` locally you need the real secret, `localhost` in
# TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES **in a local file only**, and a browser to solve the challenge.
#
# THE TRAP: a dummy sitekey mints the token XXXX.DUMMY.TOKEN.XXXX, and only a
# dummy secret accepts it — production secret keys reject dummy tokens. The dummy
# pair can never stand in for the real one in production, and a build that
# silently accepts a dummy token looks protected and is not.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=""
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=""
TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES="rnui.dev"

# notify-and-preview ticket 06 — double opt-in.
#
# SUBSCRIBE_TOKEN_SECRET signs the confirmation token. `allow create` on the
# signup collection is open by necessity (the write uses the same public client
# SDK a browser does), so without this signature anyone could plant a pending
# record at a document id they chose and confirm it, injecting an address into
# the Resend audience that was never mailed. Generate with:
#   node -e 'console.log(require("crypto").randomBytes(32).toString("base64url"))'
# Never NEXT_PUBLIC_. Set a DIFFERENT value per environment on Vercel.
SUBSCRIBE_TOKEN_SECRET=""

# The origin the confirmation link points at. Pinned rather than read from the
# Host header, which is attacker-controlled — otherwise a forged Host makes the
# site mail a real person a link to somebody else's domain, over rnui.dev's own
# authenticated sender. Leave unset locally to fall back to the request host.
# Production: https://rnui.dev   Preview: https://preview.rnui.dev
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_ORIGIN=""
